# Line endings.
#
# Every check in `npm run build` reads the tree as text: check-version.mjs
# matches `name = "onyx"\nversion = "…"` in Cargo.lock, check-ipc.mjs and
# check-theme.mjs split sources on "\n", check-ab-parity.mjs matches multi-line
# patterns with a literal \n in them. On a Windows checkout with Git's default
# core.autocrlf=true every one of those sees \r\n and fails — which is exactly
# what broke the Windows half of the v1.0.0 release build, before cargo was
# ever reached.
#
# Normalise to LF in the repository *and* in the working tree on every
# platform. Rust, Node, Vite and MSVC are all happy with LF on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts that are executed by their host shell.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.sf2   binary
*.wav   binary
*.aiff  binary
*.aif   binary
*.caf   binary
*.flac  binary
*.mp3   binary
*.m4a   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.opus  binary
*.mka   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.mov   binary
*.webm  binary

# Generated / vendored: keep them out of diffs and language stats.
package-lock.json  -diff linguist-generated
Cargo.lock         -diff linguist-generated

# Vendored third-party licence text, bundled into the app as a resource. It is
# stored with CRLF exactly as its author shipped it; `-text` keeps it that way
# rather than letting the rule above renormalise someone else's document.
crates/onyx-core/assets/gm/GeneralUser-GS-LICENSE.txt -text
